  "candidates": [
    {
      "moveId": "pro-quantitative-brain-cannot-deduce-experience",
      "type": "fallacy",
      "label": "Argument from ignorance",
      "blindReviews": [
        {
          "pass": "review-a",
          "decision": "accepted",
          "rationale": "The move advances from the absence of a present inferential route from quantitative brain parameters to experience to the categorical claim that materialists cannot supply one even in principle. The assessment expressly identifies that unsupported modal escalation."
        },
        {
          "pass": "review-b",
          "decision": "accepted",
          "rationale": "The move advances from the absence of any presently supplied deduction to the categorical claim that materialists cannot provide one even in principle. The assessment expressly identifies that unsupported modal step."
        }
      ],
      "definition": {
        "type": "fallacy",
        "slug": "argument-from-ignorance",
        "label": "Argument from ignorance",
        "definition": "Treating a claim as true because it has not been disproved, or false because it has not been proved, rather than supplying positive evidence.",
        "url": "https://logfall.com/fallacies/argument-from-ignorance/"
      },
      "moveEvidence": {
        "moveId": "pro-quantitative-brain-cannot-deduce-experience",
        "side": "pro",
        "sectionTitle": "The Hard Problem, Emergence, and Missing Knowledge",
        "role": "Constructive",
        "currentDescription": "Quantitative brain descriptions provide no inferential route to qualitative experience; therefore materialism faces a foundational explanatory gap that idealism claims to avoid.",
        "lockedProposition": "Materialism cannot deduce qualitative experience from the quantitative parameters used to describe brains, creating the hard problem.",
        "completeLockedSourceExcerpt": "Consciousness materialists cannot find a way not even in principle to deduce the qualities of experience like what it is like to taste a strawberry or to have a belly ache to deduce these qualities from the the quantitative parameters uh that uh that characterize for instance our brains there is nothing about the material brain in terms of which we could deduce our inner experience and I",
        "assessmentRationaleWithoutScores": "Strongest feature: Kastrup precisely contrasts quantitatively specified neural parameters with unmistakably qualitative phenomena such as strawberry taste and abdominal pain, thereby identifying the motion’s central comparative explanatory burden. Principal limitation: His categorical claim that materialists cannot succeed even in principle exceeds the supplied demonstration, because the excerpt establishes present nondeduction without conclusively excluding undiscovered principles or richer conceptions of matter. Live burden: He must show that quantitative characterization is constitutive of materialism rather than merely characteristic of current physical models, while answering Jump’s possibility that missing knowledge could eventually connect the domains."
      }
    },
    {
      "moveId": "con-cd-analogy-supports-missing-framework",
      "type": "fallacy",
      "label": "Argument from ignorance",
      "blindReviews": [
        {
          "pass": "review-a",
          "decision": "rejected",
          "rationale": "The appeal to missing decoding knowledge is a serious candidate because it invokes what may remain unknown, but the move only rebuts an impossibility inference and preserves a possibility. It does not treat material emergence as true merely because it has not been disproved."
        },
        {
          "pass": "review-b",
          "decision": "rejected",
          "rationale": "The analogy is weak positive support for epistemic possibility, but the move does not claim material emergence is true merely because it remains unrefuted. Its stated conclusion is only that present failure does not establish impossibility."
        }
      ],
      "definition": {
        "type": "fallacy",
        "slug": "argument-from-ignorance",
        "label": "Argument from ignorance",
        "definition": "Treating a claim as true because it has not been disproved, or false because it has not been proved, rather than supplying positive evidence.",
        "url": "https://logfall.com/fallacies/argument-from-ignorance/"
      },
      "moveEvidence": {
        "moveId": "con-cd-analogy-supports-missing-framework",
        "side": "con",
        "sectionTitle": "The Hard Problem, Emergence, and Missing Knowledge",
        "role": "Direct reply",
        "currentDescription": "Failure to reconstruct a compact disc from heard music may reflect missing decoding knowledge; likewise, present ignorance need not make material consciousness impossible.",
        "lockedProposition": "The CD analogy suggests that lacking the decoding framework needed to reverse-engineer a source does not establish that such reconstruction is impossible.",
        "completeLockedSourceExcerpt": "CD until you actually know the the programming language of the computer and then how it's read on a CD and so there's no combination of things that could be on the sound that you listen to from the music that you could use to reverse engineer the CD but that doesn't mean it's impossible to go from the sound to the CD and the same could be true of conscious experience the fact",
        "assessmentRationaleWithoutScores": "Strongest feature: Jump’s compact-disc comparison directly separates current inability from principled impossibility, supporting his deliberately limited conclusion that materialism can remain viable despite lacking a completed derivation of experience. Principal limitation: The analogy concerns transformations among publicly measurable structures, whereas Kastrup’s objection concerns the transition from such structures to felt qualities, so the relevant correspondence is not established. Live burden: Jump must explain why consciousness resembles an undiscovered decoding relation rather than a categorical difference, without treating mere conceivable future knowledge as positive evidence for material emergence."
      }
    },
    {
      "moveId": "pro-physical-chain-does-not-reduce-melody",
      "type": "fallacy",
      "label": "Argument from ignorance",
      "blindReviews": [
        {
          "pass": "review-a",
          "decision": "accepted",
          "rationale": "After showing that the experienced melody is absent from the reconstructed physical chain, the move treats that conceptual nondeduction as establishing nonreducibility. The assessment specifically says this does not independently exclude an expanded physical account."
        },
        {
          "pass": "review-b",
          "decision": "accepted",
          "rationale": "The move treats the failure to derive experienced melody from the described physical chain as establishing its irreducibility. The assessment specifically says conceptual nondeduction does not exclude an expanded physical account."
        }
      ],
      "definition": {
        "type": "fallacy",
        "slug": "argument-from-ignorance",
        "label": "Argument from ignorance",
        "definition": "Treating a claim as true because it has not been disproved, or false because it has not been proved, rather than supplying positive evidence.",
        "url": "https://logfall.com/fallacies/argument-from-ignorance/"
      },
      "moveEvidence": {
        "moveId": "pro-physical-chain-does-not-reduce-melody",
        "side": "pro",
        "sectionTitle": "The Hard Problem, Emergence, and Missing Knowledge",
        "role": "Direct reply",
        "currentDescription": "Although air-pressure patterns reduce through the disc and sound system, the consciously heard melody does not; thus Jump’s analogy leaves experience unexplained.",
        "lockedProposition": "Physical pressure oscillations can be reduced through the CD system, but the experienced melody cannot be reduced to that physical chain.",
        "completeLockedSourceExcerpt": "the patterns of pressure oscillations in the air which is what it is from a materialist perspective you can surely reduce that to your CD to the to the grooves that you see on your CD if you would amplify that uh to the mechanisms electronic mechanisms in the CD to the speaker it's entirely reducible what is not reducible is when you say the Melody I hear and I experience I cannot reduce that to the CD and the sound system with",
        "assessmentRationaleWithoutScores": "Strongest feature: Kastrup grants everything the compact-disc analogy successfully demonstrates about physical signals, then isolates the experienced melody as the element absent from the reconstructed mechanical chain. Principal limitation: The reply clarifies the explanatory target but still relies on conceptual nondeduction rather than independently establishing that no expanded physical account could ever incorporate phenomenal character. Live burden: Jump must now identify a bridge from signal processing to hearing, while Kastrup must defend the stronger claim that the missing bridge reflects ontology rather than incomplete theory."
      }
    },
    {
      "moveId": "con-organized-matter-may-yield-emergence",
      "type": "fallacy",
      "label": "Begging the question",
      "blindReviews": [
        {
          "pass": "review-a",
          "decision": "accepted",
          "rationale": "The disputed conclusion—that an appropriate material combination can instantiate consciousness—is simply asserted through examples of walls and encoded sound that never contain qualitative subjectivity. The assessment says those comparisons leave the exact disputed transition untouched."
        },
        {
          "pass": "review-b",
          "decision": "rejected",
          "rationale": "The examples do not establish qualitative emergence, but the move presents an affirmative hypothesis and analogies rather than covertly assuming a premise identical to its conclusion. This is underdevelopment, not demonstrated circularity."
        }
      ],
      "definition": {
        "type": "fallacy",
        "slug": "begging-the-question",
        "label": "Begging the question",
        "definition": "Assuming, directly or indirectly, the very point that needs to be established, so the conclusion is smuggled into the premises.",
        "url": "https://logfall.com/fallacies/begging-the-question/"
      },
      "moveEvidence": {
        "moveId": "con-organized-matter-may-yield-emergence",
        "side": "con",
        "sectionTitle": "The Hard Problem, Emergence, and Missing Knowledge",
        "role": "Direct reply",
        "currentDescription": "Appropriately organized matter might yield consciousness as combinations yield walls or digital sound; hence material emergence remains possible despite present explanatory incompleteness.",
        "lockedProposition": "Consciousness can be an emergent property of an appropriately organized material medium, just as combinations yield higher-level structures or outputs.",
        "completeLockedSourceExcerpt": "well I don't deny Consciousness exist I just think Consciousness is a emergent property of matter so I don't Define matter as being ontologically separate from Consciousness I believe that there is a combination of matter that can get Consciousness just like there's a combination of bricks that gets a wall and a combination of ones and zeros that can get sound you just have to find the right medium so I mean your argument",
        "assessmentRationaleWithoutScores": "Strongest feature: Jump clearly states his affirmative materialist alternative: consciousness need not be denied or ontologically separated from matter if suitable organization can instantiate it as an emergent property. Principal limitation: Walls from bricks and sound from binary patterns illustrate structural composition or encoded output, not the production of qualitative subjectivity, so they leave the exact disputed transition untouched. Live burden: He must specify which material organization matters and explain how its activity entails experience, rather than saying researchers merely need to discover the correct medium."
      }
    },
    {
      "moveId": "pro-quantitative-matter-cannot-bridge-quality",
      "type": "fallacy",
      "label": "Begging the question",
      "blindReviews": [
        {
          "pass": "review-a",
          "decision": "accepted",
          "rationale": "The conclusion of incommensurability is obtained by defining matter as exhaustively quantitative, although whether material reality is exhausted by current quantitative characterization is itself the disputed premise. The assessment explicitly identifies this definitional dependence."
        },
        {
          "pass": "review-b",
          "decision": "accepted",
          "rationale": "The conclusion of incommensurability is secured by defining material reality as exhaustively quantitative, the very characterization disputed by the opposing ontology. The assessment says this stipulates away unknown relevant properties."
        }
      ],
      "definition": {
        "type": "fallacy",
        "slug": "begging-the-question",
        "label": "Begging the question",
        "definition": "Assuming, directly or indirectly, the very point that needs to be established, so the conclusion is smuggled into the premises.",
        "url": "https://logfall.com/fallacies/begging-the-question/"
      },
      "moveEvidence": {
        "moveId": "pro-quantitative-matter-cannot-bridge-quality",
        "side": "pro",
        "sectionTitle": "The Hard Problem, Emergence, and Missing Knowledge",
        "role": "Direct reply",
        "currentDescription": "If matter is exhaustively characterized through quantities, its properties cannot entail experiential qualities; consequently, asserting an eventual reduction does not provide the missing bridge.",
        "lockedProposition": "If matter is exhaustively quantitative, it is fundamentally incommensurable with qualitative experience, so merely asserting reduction does not bridge the gap.",
        "completeLockedSourceExcerpt": "matter is defined as something that is exhaustively described in quantitative ways I exhaustively describe material arrangement in ter in terms of mass char March momentum geometric relationships uh field interactions frequencies amplitudes if you define matter that way it's fundamentally Inc commentable with the qualities of experience so to say that well it mind is reducible to matter we just haven't found a way to do that",
        "assessmentRationaleWithoutScores": "Strongest feature: Kastrup makes his impossibility argument explicit by listing mass, momentum, geometry, frequency, and amplitude, then contrasting those relational quantities with the intrinsic qualities requiring explanation. Principal limitation: The conclusion depends decisively on defining material reality as exhaustively quantitative, a characterization Jump disputes by distinguishing physical description from matter’s potentially richer ontology. Live burden: Kastrup must defend that definition against materialists who treat present physics as incomplete, rather than obtaining incommensurability by stipulating away unknown consciousness-relevant properties."
      }
    },
    {
      "moveId": "con-active-process-may-produce-consciousness",
      "type": "fallacy",
      "label": "Begging the question",
      "blindReviews": [
        {
          "pass": "review-a",
          "decision": "accepted",
          "rationale": "The move improves the candidate mechanism from aggregation to active process, but then says the process produces consciousness without identifying any consciousness-relevant process or showing why it entails experience. The central production claim is therefore assumed rather than established."
        },
        {
          "pass": "review-b",
          "decision": "rejected",
          "rationale": "The move fails to identify the relevant process or explain why it entails experience, but proposing process as a causal hypothesis does not itself smuggle the conclusion into a premise. The recorded defect is insufficient specification."
        }
      ],
      "definition": {
        "type": "fallacy",
        "slug": "begging-the-question",
        "label": "Begging the question",
        "definition": "Assuming, directly or indirectly, the very point that needs to be established, so the conclusion is smuggled into the premises.",
        "url": "https://logfall.com/fallacies/begging-the-question/"
      },
      "moveEvidence": {
        "moveId": "con-active-process-may-produce-consciousness",
        "side": "con",
        "sectionTitle": "The Hard Problem, Emergence, and Missing Knowledge",
        "role": "Constructive",
        "currentDescription": "Consciousness may depend on a system’s active internal process rather than its component count or static complexity, as living and dead bodies differ dynamically.",
        "lockedProposition": "Consciousness may arise from a system's active process rather than its number of parts or static complexity, as the contrast between living and dead bodies suggests.",
        "completeLockedSourceExcerpt": "no 10,000 I don't think the number is what's important I think it's the process within them so like if I look at a dead body I think that's not conscious but if you pass electricity through it then I think that it could be conscious because of not the number or complexity of the system but the process within the system so I think it's the process that's going on within the system that produces the Consciousness not the size",
        "assessmentRationaleWithoutScores": "Strongest feature: Jump shifts the materialist proposal from crude aggregation to dynamic organization, recognizing that the relevant candidate is an active process rather than the mere number or complexity of components. Principal limitation: Passing electricity through dead tissue is insufficiently discriminating, and the source never specifies which process produces consciousness or why its operation entails qualitative experience. Live burden: He must identify consciousness-relevant dynamics and connect them to subjectivity, not merely note that living conscious organisms differ causally from inactive bodies."
      }
    },
    {
      "moveId": "pro-strong-emergence-lacks-deduction",
      "type": "fallacy",
      "label": "Argument from ignorance",
      "blindReviews": [
        {
          "pass": "review-a",
          "decision": "accepted",
          "rationale": "The move uses the absence of a presently accepted derivation of experiential qualities to support the stronger conclusion encoded by its treatment of strong emergence. The assessment expressly warns that no derivation presented so far does not prove every future material derivation impossible."
        },
