{"moveId":"pro-strong-emergence-lacks-deduction","type":"fallacy","label":"Argument from ignorance","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The move uses the absence of a presently accepted derivation of experiential qualities to support the stronger conclusion encoded by its treatment of strong emergence. The assessment expressly warns that no derivation presented so far does not prove every future material derivation impossible."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"accepted","rationale":"The move uses the lack of any currently presented or accepted rationale for deriving experience to support a stronger claim of nonderivability. The assessment explicitly states that present absence does not prove future impossibility."}],"definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"argument-from-ignorance","label":"Argument from ignorance","definition":"Treating a claim as true because it has not been disproved, or false because it has not been proved, rather than supplying positive evidence.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/argument-from-ignorance/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-strong-emergence-lacks-deduction","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"The Hard Problem, Emergence, and Missing Knowledge","role":"Direct reply","currentDescription":"Ordinary emergence is derivable from component behavior, whereas calling consciousness strongly emergent acknowledges that no rationale presently derives experiential qualities from material characteristics.","lockedProposition":"Ordinary emergent properties are deducible from system components, whereas consciousness is called strongly emergent because no rationale derives experiential qualities from component characteristics.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"these cases you can deduce the emergent property from the system components but the claim of the heart problem and the challenge of the heart problem is that Consciousness is not weakly emergent it's strongly emergent which is a code for we have no idea how it happens because nobody has put forward a a rationale for deducing the qualities of experience at least in principle from the characteristics of the components of","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup distinguishes ordinary, deducible higher-level behavior from the unexplained appearance of qualitative experience, directly answering both Jump’s composition examples and his appeal to active process. Principal limitation: Showing that no derivation was presented, or is currently accepted, does not conclusively demonstrate that every future material derivation is impossible. Live burden: Jump must offer more than the label “process,” while Kastrup must justify his stronger modal conclusion without overlooking material ontologies that include presently unknown intrinsic properties."}}
{"moveId":"pro-matter-is-appearance-of-mental-states","type":"fallacy","label":"Begging the question","blindReviews":[{"pass":"review-a","decision":"rejected","rationale":"The universal extension from brains to inanimate matter is weakly supported and depends on an undefended transpersonal subject, making this a close circularity candidate. It is rejected because the move presents the extension as a proposed positive ontology rather than using it covertly as proof of itself."},{"pass":"review-b","decision":"rejected","rationale":"The universal extension requires independent support for a transpersonal subject, but merely stating a preferred interpretation is underargued rather than formally circular. The assessment does not show the conclusion embedded in a premise."}],"definition":{"type":"fallacy","slug":"begging-the-question","label":"Begging the question","definition":"Assuming, directly or indirectly, the very point that needs to be established, so the conclusion is smuggled into the premises.","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/begging-the-question/"},"moveEvidence":{"moveId":"pro-matter-is-appearance-of-mental-states","side":"pro","sectionTitle":"Matter, Scientific Description, and Ontological Starting Points","role":"Constructive","currentDescription":"Because brains outwardly present inner experience, Kastrup extends that relation universally, concluding that matter is the observable appearance of personal or transpersonal mentality.","lockedProposition":"Idealism treats brains and inanimate matter as observable appearances of personal and transpersonal mental states.","completeLockedSourceExcerpt":"and it's it appears from our perspective in the form that we call matter just like my brain appears to you in the form that you call matter but my brain in my view is just the way my inner experience looks like uh from a second or third person perspective and I think the same goes for the inanimate Universe the matter in the inanimate universe is just what transpersonal uh mental States look","assessmentRationaleWithoutScores":"Strongest feature: Kastrup supplies a positive ontology rather than merely criticizing materialism, using the familiar contrast between privately lived experience and another person’s publicly observable brain to explain what material appearance means. Principal limitation: Extending that personal appearance relation to the entire inanimate universe requires an independently defended transpersonal subject, whereas the source principally states the extension as his preferred interpretation. Live burden: He must establish why the brain analogy licenses universalization and why a shared material world is better understood as mentality’s appearance than as evidence for a mind-independent substrate."}}
